{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST bundle a `habeas-refactor` skill whose description tells agents to\nuse it when a change moves code, tests, or requirements to another file,\nchapter, or outline node, including splitting or merging modules or chapters,\nor renames a requirement, so that evidence whose content is unchanged is not\ninvalidated.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has these obligations: Habeas bundles a `habeas-refactor` skill, and its description tells agents to use it when a change moves code, tests, or requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node. The description must also cover splitting or merging modules or chapters, renaming a requirement, and the purpose that unchanged evidence is not invalidated.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_is_used_for_moves_and_requirement_renames]` is marked {test}. It reads the skill's front-matter description through the helper `description`. The helper calls `skill_md`, which calls `skill_files`. `skill_files` embeds `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md` with `include_str!`. That shows the skill exists as a bundled asset and fails at compile time if the file is missing. The test asserts that the description contains each of four phrases:\n- \"Use when a change moves code, tests, or requirements in a Habeas project to another file, chapter, or outline node\"\n- \"splitting or merging modules or chapters\"\n- \"moving or renaming a requirement\"\n- \"so that evidence whose content is unchanged is not invalidated\"\n\nTogether these cover every clause of the description obligation: the move triggers, the splitting and merging case, the requirement rename, and the not-invalidated purpose.\n\nThe `habeas-refactor` skill is also in the `SKILLS` constant, which I only saw in a search snippet. Other tests in the file also use the skill, including `bundled_skill_descriptions_say_when_to_use_them`, but I relied on the marked test for this judgment.\n\nI found no obligation without an assertion, and no wrong expected result. The tests would catch a missing skill, a missing description, or a description that dropped any of the required cases. There are no acknowledgments.",
  "references": "37d40bf95bcce56214c52022ef9a83596776e98170a6ddcfc2452d99d6984cdf",
